edit(p,'at every moment, *some* thread completes its operation in a bounded number of steps. An individual thread can starve, but the system as a whole never stalls.','continued execution guarantees system-wide operation progress under the algorithm\'s primitive and scheduling assumptions. An individual operation can starve; this is not a fixed wall-clock completion deadline.', 'Lockfreeprogressnotateverymomentfinitewalltime.',haz)
edit(p,'alternatively a small counter is packed into the unused high bits of a 64-bit pointer, which works but wraps around after relatively few updates (65,536 with 16 bits).','packing a small counter into pointer bits is platform-specific and requires validating address representation and atomic support. A 16-bit tag wraps after 65536 updates; any finite tag needs an argument that wraparound cannot alias an outstanding observation.', 'PointerhighbitsnotuniversallyunusedandfiniteABAremains.',s)
edit(p,'C++26 standardises both hazard pointers and RCU, a sign of how fundamental the reclamation problem is.','WG21 proposals P2530 and P2545 specify hazard-pointer and RCU facilities for C++; check the chosen standard-library implementation before relying on availability.', 'Versionavailabilitynotprovenbypaperalone.',haz+['https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2023/p2545r4.pdf'])
edit(p,'If the read section is a few hundred nanoseconds, a read-write lock usually scales no better than a plain mutex, and costs more per operation.','For sufficiently short reads, this overhead can outweigh reader parallelism; there is no universal duration threshold.', 'Unsupportedperformancethreshold.',rcu)
edit(p,'Read-write locks pay off when read sections are **long** (microseconds or more, for example walking a large structure) and writes are rare.','Longer read sections and rare writes can make reader parallelism worthwhile, but the crossover must be measured on the actual implementation and hardware.', 'Universal microsecondthresholdunsupported.',rcu)
edit(p,'(Real implementations need careful memory barriers here, and the reader\'s data reads must be atomic to avoid a C data race; the Linux kernel\'s `seqlock_t` provides them.)','This is algorithm pseudocode. Portable C requires a race-free representation (for example atomic fields) plus a proven ordering protocol; retrying does not undo undefined behavior. Linux seqlock APIs follow the Linux kernel memory model and do not magically make arbitrary plain C fields portable atomic objects. Writers must also meet the API\'s serialization and non-preemption requirements.', 'CriticalkernelmemorymodelnotISOatomics.',seq+s)
edit(p,'A writer never modifies data that readers might be looking at. Instead it:','In this immutable-configuration example, a writer does not modify a published object. Instead it:', 'RCUcaninplaceupdateswithadditionalprotocol.',rcu)
edit(p,'        malloc(sizeof *new);\n    *new = *old;','        malloc(sizeof *new);\n    if (!new) return;  // report failure\n    *new = *old;','AvoidNULLdereference evenillustrative.',s)
edit(p,'`rcu_assign_pointer` is a release store','This is RCU-style pseudocode: a real kernel implementation uses kernel allocation and protected pointer-access APIs; a liburcu application needs its selected flavor\'s headers, registration and lifecycle rules. Assume cur initially refers to a valid object and writers hold the same mutex.\n\n`rcu_assign_pointer` is a release store','MixedmallockernelAPInotstandalonecode.',rcu)
edit(p,'The grace period only has to cover readers that started *before* the publish (R1, R2). R3 started after it and can only find the new version, so the writer does not wait for it.','synchronize_rcu waits for read-side sections that pre-exist its invocation, not necessarily every later reader. In the simplified diagram publication and the start of the grace-period wait are adjacent; R1/R2 may retain the old object. Actual grace-period completion need not coincide with the last illustrated unlock.', 'APIboundaryinvocationnotarbitrarypublish.',rcu)
edit(p,'Readers pay almost zero, and the cost moves entirely to writers, whose grace periods typically take milliseconds.','Costs and grace-period duration depend on RCU flavor, configuration and workload; no fixed timing is supplied here.', 'Unsupportedzero/entirecost/timing.',rcu)
edit(p,'Garbage-collected languages get the hard part, the grace period, for free. Readers take a reference to an immutable object; writers build a new one and swap a single reference; the old object is freed only when its last reader drops it.','Managed runtimes help keep referenced objects alive, but do not supply publication ordering or application immutability automatically. This example uses a brief shared lock to obtain or publish a snapshot, then allows use of that immutable snapshot outside the lock.', 'GCnotfreeRCU/properpublishingneeded.',py)
edit(p,'    return _config   # one atomic read','    with _write_lock:\n        return _config  # immutable snapshot','Portable explicitpublicationprotocol.',py)
edit(p,'    with _write_lock:      # writers only','    with _write_lock:      # publish lock','Readersnowparticipate.',py)
edit(p,'Readers take no lock. A reader that fetched the old dict keeps a consistent snapshot; reference counting frees it when they finish.','Readers briefly take the publication lock, then retain a reference to an unchanged dictionary. Reclamation timing is runtime-dependent. Callers must not mutate the returned dictionary or mutable objects reachable from it.', 'Nolockportableclaimremoved andshallowimmutability.',py)
edit(p,'would put readers back into a data race.','could violate the snapshot invariant; do not equate Python compound-operation races with the precise C data-race definition.', 'Languageleveldistinction.',py)
edit(p,'## Choosing a tool','> [!note] Content gap\n> No hardware-specific lock, cache-transfer or RCU timing benchmark is available. The stack deliberately omits safe reclamation, and the seqlock/RCU fragments are protocol illustrations, not complete production implementations.\n\n## Choosing a tool','Explicitmissingmeasurement/implementation.',rcu)
qe(q,'sync-lock-free-q5','prompt','Assume every node removed from a C Treiber stack is retired and never explicitly reinserted while stale readers can retain it. Which techniques can address address-reuse ABA when implemented correctly? Assume a version tag cannot wrap while an observation is outstanding.','Hazardsalonecannotpreventexplicitreinsertion;tagwrap.',haz)
qe(q,'sync-lock-free-q5','text','Hazard pointers with publish-then-revalidate before dereference, and retirement that forbids reclamation/reuse while protected','Publishingwithoutrecheckuseafterfree.',haz,option=3)
qe(q,'sync-lock-free-q6','workedExample','A plausible bottleneck is atomic modification of shared rwlock metadata on reader entry/exit. That coherence traffic can dominate very short useful work. The hypothetical 100 ns lookup is not a measured cache-transfer latency, and write frequency alone does not prove writer hold times are harmless. Profile the implementation before choosing RCU, sharding or another protocol.','Remove invented100nscoherencetiming anduniversaldiagnosis.',rcu)
qe(q,'sync-lock-free-q6','explanation','A low write count does not alone rule out long writer hold times. The question illustrates reader-metadata contention as a plausible cause; measurement must distinguish it from writer delays.','Countfractionnotduration.',rcu,option=1)
qe(q,'sync-lock-free-q8','explanation','Even if an individual operation has runtime-level protection, a pair of updates is not a portable atomic snapshot. The invariant requires immutable publication or synchronized reads of the entire pair.','GILsingleassignmentnotuniversalcallback guarantee.',py,option=0)
qe(q,'sync-lock-free-q8','explanation','get_config now takes a brief publication lock. That does not deadlock this protocol; the defect is that returned dictionaries may be mutated later while readers use them outside the lock.','Matchcorrectedlesson.',py,option=1)
qe(q,'sync-lock-free-q9','workedExample','At the synchronize_rcu invocation (t=3), R1 has already exited and R2 is active. R2 must finish before the wait may complete. R3 starts afterward and is not necessarily included. Thus t=5 is a lower bound, not a promised return time; batching and scheduling can delay completion.','UseformalAPIboundary and noexacttiming.',rcu)
qe(q,'sync-lock-free-q11','explanation','A 64-bit tag wraps after 2^64 updates: about 584.5 years at 10^9 updates/s. This illustrates a very long cycle, but finite tags are not a mathematical no-wrap guarantee. The immediate dereference-after-free bug needs no wraparound.','QualifyfiniteABAprotection.',s,option=1)
qe(q,'sync-lock-free-q12','prompt','A hypothetical 32-bit kernel reads a split 64-bit timestamp frequently and updates it in a short timer-interrupt section. Assume a proven kernel memory-model implementation, serialized non-preempted writers and retry-safe reads. Which listed technique is a good fit without allocating a new object per update?','AvoidISOplainracingseqlockappearsvalid; hypotheticalloadnotbenchmark.',seq)
